title: Working together beats one mind when the work splits into parts with no decision in common, or when the best decision needs facts only another holds.
pid: 90ec678e-bb82-4178-b30c-063362a90dec
body: |2-
  Read: Google's study of 180 agent systems (Kim et al. 2025) found
  coordinated agents 81% better than one on a task that splits into
  parallel parts; Anthropic's research system gains most on
  breadth-first questions. Read in summaries: in the "hidden profile"
  experiments (Stasser and Titus 1985) the best choice is visible only
  when each member's unique facts are pooled, and groups rarely find it.
  Ours, from the first use: three specialists built the four parts of
  Vic's plan, one after another, each in its own territory, and it
  worked; Drew answered a question in his building mission that Vic's
  plan could not.
